**3.3 Renal Outcomes and Nephroprotection** 

The renoprotective effects of GLP-1 RAs are robust and increasingly recognized as a primary therapeutic target. Pooled analyses and recently completed dedicated renal outcomes trials (most notably the FLOW trial assessing semaglutide 1.0 mg) indicate a highly significant reduction in composite kidney outcomes. These outcomes include a sustained eGFR decline of greater than 50%, progression to end-stage renal disease (ESRD), and death attributable to renal causes. The cardiovascular and renal protective effects are synergistic; mitigating renal decline inherently reduces the risk of heart failure hospitalizations, which is vital in complex diabetic patients.

**3.4 Hepatology: Metabolic Dysfunction-Associated Steatohepatitis (MASH)** 

Concurrently, hepatology has emerged as a major novel therapeutic frontier for GLP-1 RAs. Patients presenting with T2DM and obesity frequently exhibit metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease (MASLD), which aggressively progresses to MASH—a progressive inflammatory fibrotic liver disease leading directly to cirrhosis, portal hypertension, and hepatocellular carcinoma.

The Phase 3 ESSENCE trial evaluated the administration of semaglutide 2.4 mg in patients with noncirrhotic MASH accompanied by moderate-to-advanced liver fibrosis. At the 72-week interim analysis, 63% of patients receiving semaglutide achieved MASH resolution without any worsening of fibrosis, compared to only 34% in the placebo cohort. Furthermore, 37% of the semaglutide cohort achieved definitive improvement in liver scarring without worsening of MASH, compared to 22% in the placebo group. This highly robust histological evidence facilitated the FDA's decision to grant accelerated approval for semaglutide in this specific indication in late 2025, validating the agent's capability to reverse hepatic inflammatory architecture and establish GLP-1 RAs as disease-modifying agents in hepatology.

